NAME
    globx - expand a filename glob into a list of paths
            (//#use globx, ;#use globx)

SYNOPSIS
    //#use glob         (required ABOVE globx -- provides gmatch())
    //#use globx        C:   cc splices /lib/lib_globx.c
    ;#use glob
    ;#use globx         asm: mkasm.sh appends /lib/lib_globx.inc

DESCRIPTION
    For multi-file commands (cat *.asm, cp/mv, and the stdin helper's
    wc/grep/sort): given a pattern whose LAST component may contain * or
    ?, scan the directory it names (or the CWD) and collect the FILES
    (not subdirectories) whose name matches, as a list of ready-to-open
    paths.

    glob_expand(pat, out, maxn) -> count
      pat    the glob, e.g. "*.ASM" (CWD) or "/BIN/*.BIN" (a named dir)
      out    caller buffer of maxn fixed 64-byte slots; out[i*64] = the
             i-th path (the pattern's directory prefix + the matched
             name, so it opens the same way the bare argument would --
             relative names resolve in the CWD)
      maxn   capacity in slots; matches past maxn are dropped
      ->     number of matches written (0..maxn)

    DEPENDS on glob's gmatch() -- `//#use glob` must appear ABOVE
    `//#use globx`. It iterates with FNEXT on scratch page $FA (a
    512-byte sector just below the file read buffer at $FC00 and clear of
    the code/globals), and finishes building the whole path list before
    any file is opened -- so sharing the high TPA with the $FC00 read
    buffer is safe (the two are never live at once).

    Not pulled into dir/find (they only //#use glob), so it does not
    bloat those size-tight recursive commands.

SEE ALSO
    glob, stdin, dirent, cat, cp, mv
